                <pb facs="0572" n="496" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg496"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/847: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-05-06T11:00:00+02:00">May 6, 1935—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 6—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>65. During the past week the Government appeared to regain control of the
                    situation and the attitude of the court in the trial of the political leaders
                    showed almost a complete change. Mavromichalis resigned on Saturday and
                    [Panayiotis] Tsaldaris resumed charge of the Foreign Office. On that day the
                    prosecuting attorneys summed up in a just and temperate manner and the verdict
                    returned yesterday, Sunday, afternoon acquits Papanastasiou, Caphandaris and
                    Mylonas entirely while giving Gonatas only 5 years imprisonment and condemning
                    to death no one but absentees like Venizelos and Plastiras. <pb facs="0573"
                        n="497" xml:id="pg497"/> Maximos is leaving today to attend the meeting of
                    the Council of the Balkan Entente in Bucharest and it is rumored that he will
                    assume the portfolio of Foreign Affairs upon his return. It is generally
                    conceded in Athens that this victory of moderation and sanity owes much to our
                    attitude as well as that of France and Britain.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
